" (https://oaei.ontologymatching.org/doc/oaei-methods.1.pdf)\nciteturn24view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 36; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn23view0\",\"id\":3}); Total lines: 891\nL0@P0: Towards a methodology for evaluating alignment\nL1@P0: and matching algorithms\nL2@P0: Version 1.0\nL3@P0: Ontology Alignment Evaluation Initiative1\nL4@P0: http://oaei.inrialpes.fr\nL5@P0: May 2, 2005\nL6@P0: 1Coordinator: Jérôme Euzenat (INRIA Rhône-Alpes) with contributions of Marc Ehrig\nL7@P0-1: (Universität Karlruhe), and Raúl García Castro (UP Madrid)Abstract\nL8@P1: This document considers the potential strategies for experimentally evaluating on\u0002tology alignment algorithms. It first identifies various goals for such an evaluation,\nL9@P1: the most important objective being the improvement of existing methods. It con\u0002siders the various parameters of the alignment task that must be controlled during\nL10@P1: the experiment and examine the measures that can be used for an evaluation. It\nL11@P1: then propose a framework for organising the evaluation based on some principles\nL12@P1-2: and efforts that have already been undergone in the specific field of ontology align\u0002ment.Executive Summary\nL13@P2: Heterogeneity problems on the semantic web can be solved, for some of them,\nL14@P2: by aligning or matching heterogeneous ontologies. Aligning ontologies consists\nL15@P2: of finding the corresponding entities in these ontologies. Many techniques are\nL16@P2: available for achieving ontology alignment and many systems have been devel\u0002oped based on these techniques. However, few comparisons and few integration is\nL17@P2: actually provided by these implementations.\nL18@P2: The present report studies what kind of evaluation can be carried out on align\u0002ment algorithms in order to help the worldwide research community to improve on\nL19@P2: the current techniques. It should be considered as a white paper describing what\nL20@P2: the Ontology Alignment Evaluation Initiative is supposed to be.\nL21@P2: In this document, we first examine the purpose and types of evaluation as well\nL22@P2: as established evaluation methodology (§1). We found that two different kinds\nL23@P2: of benchmarks are worth developing for ontology alignment: competence bench\u0002marks based on many “unit tests” which characterise a particular situation and\nL24@P2: enable to assess the capabilities of each algorithms and performance benchmarks\nL25@P2: based on challenging “real-world” situations in which algorithms are in competi\u0002tion.\nL26@P2: We have examined the possible variations of the ontology alignment problem\nL27@P2: (§2) and the possible measures that can be used for evaluating alignment results\nL28@P2: (§3). This allows us to specify the profile of the kind of benchmarks to be per\u0002formed and how results will be evaluated. The variation opportunities are very\nL29@P2: large so we had to restrict the considered task (at least for competence benchmarks)\nL30@P2: drastically. These restrictions could be relaxed in further evaluation or when con\u0002sidering and evaluating algorithms on a particular, clearly identified subtask. Con\u0002cerning the evaluation measure, precision and recall are, so far, the best understood\nL31@P2: measures. However, it will be very important in the future to involve resource\nL32@P2: consumption measures.\nL33@P2: Then we draw on previous experiments in order to design some guidelines for\nL34@P2: performing an evaluation campaign. This involves defining a set of rules for the\nL35@P2-3: evaluation (§4).The description of the retained systematic competence benchmark tests as well\nL36@P3: as the effective rules of the evaluation campaigns will be the subject of other doc\u0002uments.\nL37@P3-4: 0Contents\nL38@P4: 1 Introduction: purpose, method and types of evaluation for ontology\nL39@P4: alignment 2\nL40@P4: 1.1 Goal of evaluation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 3\nL41@P4: 1.2 Evaluation methodology . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 3\nL42@P4: 1.3 Examples of evaluations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 5\nL43@P4: 1.4 Types of evaluations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6\nL44@P4: 1.5 Conclusion . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 8\nL45@P4: 2 Dimensions and variability of alignment evaluation 10\nL46@P4: 2.1 Input ontologies . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 11\nL47@P4: 2.2 Input alignment . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 12\nL48@P4: 2.3 Parameters and resources . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 12\nL49@P4: 2.4 Output alignment . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 14\nL50@P4: 2.5 Alignment process . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 15\nL51@P4: 2.6 Conclusion . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 17\nL52@P4: 3 Evaluation measures 18\nL53@P4: 3.1 Compliance measures . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 18\nL54@P4: 3.2 Performance measures . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 21\nL55@P4: 3.3 User-related measures . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 22\nL56@P4: 3.4 Aggregated measures . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 23\nL57@P4: 3.5 Task specific evaluation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 24\nL58@P4: 3.6 Conclusion . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 24\nL59@P4: 4 Organizational guidelines 25\nL60@P4: 4.1 Principles . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 25\nL61@P4: 4.2 Proposed implementation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 27\nL62@P4: 4.3 Conclusion . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 29\nL63@P4-5: 1Chapter 1\nL64@P5: Introduction: purpose, method\nL65@P5: and types of evaluation for\nL66@P5: ontology alignment\nL67@P5: When applications and agents use heterogeneous ontologies, it is necessary to rec\u0002onciliate these ontologies before they interoperate. This can be achieved through\nL68@P5: the alignment or matching of the ontologies.\nL69@P5: Aligning ontologies consists of finding the corresponding entities in these on\u0002tologies. There have been many different techniques proposed for implementing\nL70@P5: this process. They can be classified along the many features that can be found\nL71@P5: in ontologies (labels, structures, instances, semantics), or with regard to the kind\nL72@P5: of disciplines they belong to (e.g., statistics, combinatorics, semantics, linguistics,\nL73@P5: machine learning, or data analysis) [Rahm and Bernstein, 2001; Kalfoglou and\nL74@P5: Schorlemmer, 2003; Euzenat et al., 2004a]. The alignment itself is obtained by\nL75@P5: combining these techniques towards a particular goal (obtaining an alignment with\nL76@P5: particular features, optimising some criterion). Several combination techniques are\nL77@P5: also used.\nL78@P5: The increasing number of methods available for schema matching/ontology\nL79@P5: integration suggests the need to for evaluating of these methods. Beside their ap\u0002parent heterogeneity, it seems sensible to characterise an alignment as a set of pairs\nL80@P5: expressing the correspondences between two ontologies. Such a characterization\nL81@P5: should enable the comparison of the results provided by the algorithms.\nL82@P5: However, very few experimental comparison of algorithms are available. It is\nL83@P5: thus one of the objectives of the Ontology Alignment Evaluation Initiative to run\nL84@P5: such an evaluation.\nL85@P5-6: Since all benchmarking activity must be carried out with a systematic proce\u00022dure on clearly defined tasks, this is the purpose of this report to propose such\nL86@P6: a procedure. This introduction will define the general objective of evaluating the\nL87@P6: alignment algorithms, the overall methodology to be followed, and the kind of tests\nL88@P6: which can be performed.\nL89@P6: 1.1 Goal of evaluation\nL90@P6: The major and long term purpose of the evaluation of ontology alignment methods\nL91@P6: is to help designers and developers of such methods to improve them and to help\nL92@P6: users to evaluate the suitability of proposed methods to their needs. The bench\u0002marking considered here should help research on ontology alignment. For that\nL93@P6: purpose, the evaluation should help evaluating absolute performances (e.g., com\u0002pliance) and relative performances (e.g., in speed or accuracy).\nL94@P6: The medium term goal is to set up a set of reference benchmark tests for assess\u0002ing the strengths and weaknesses of the available tools and to compare them. Some\nL95@P6: of these tests are focussing the characterisation of the behaviour of the tools rather\nL96@P6: than having them compete on real-life problems. It is expected that they could be\nL97@P6: improved and adopted by the algorithm implementers in order to situate their al\u0002gorithms. Building benchmark suites is highly valuable not just for the group of\nL98@P6: people that participates in the contests, but for all the research community. The\nL99@P6: evaluation should thus be run over several years in order to allow the measure of\nL100@P6: the evolution of the field.\nL101@P6: The shorter term goal of the initiatives launched in 2004 was firstly to illustrate\nL102@P6: how it is possible to evaluate ontology alignment tools and to show that it was pos\u0002sible to build such an evaluation campaign. It is a common subgoal of evaluation\nL103@P6: campaign that their return helps improving the evaluation methodologies.\nL104@P6: 1.2 Evaluation methodology\nL105@P6: Each evaluation must be carried out according to some methodology. [Castro et\nL106@P6: al., 2004] presents a benchmarking methodology that is briefly summarized here.\nL107@P6: 1.2.1 Benchmarking features\nL108@P6: A benchmark is a test that measures the performances of a system or subsystem\nL109@P6: on a well defined task or set of tasks (comp.benchmark.FAQ). Evaluation should\nL110@P6: enable the measure of the degree of achievement of proposed tasks on a scale com\u0002mon to all methods. The main features of benchmarking are:\nL111@P6-7: 3– measurement via comparison: benchmarks usually measure the distance be\u0002tween a given result with some expected result (the distance can well be a\nL112@P7: yes or no answer);\nL113@P7: – continuous improvement: benchmarks should allow to monitor the improve\u0002ment (and non degradation) of a solution by running the benchmark tests\nL114@P7: again;\nL115@P7: – systematic procedure: benchmark results must be non ambiguous and their\nL116@P7: procedure reproducible.\nL117@P7: 1.2.2 Benchmarking lifecycle\nL118@P7: The benchmarking process defined in the methodology is a continuous process that\nL119@P7: should be performed indefinitely in order to obtain a continuous improvement both\nL120@P7: in the tools and in the benchmarking process itself (see Figure 1.1). This process is\nL121@P7: composed of a benchmarking iteration that is repeated forever and that is composed\nL122@P7: of three phases (Plan, Experiment, and Improve) and ends with a Recalibration\nL123@P7: task.\nL124@P7: The three phases of each iteration are the following:\nL125@P7: Plan phase It is composed of the set of tasks that must be performed for clearly\nL126@P7: refining the goal and the subject of the evaluation, preparing the proposal\nL127@P7: for benchmarking, finding other organisations that want to participate in the\nL128@P7: benchmarking activity, and planning the benchmarking.\nL129@P7: Experiment phase It is composed of the set of tasks where the experimentation\nL130@P7: over the different tools that are considered in the benchmarking activity is\nL131@P7: performed. This includes defining the experiment and its tool set, processing\nL132@P7: and analysing the data obtained, and reporting the experimentation results.\nL133@P7: Improve phase It is composed of the set of tasks where the results of the bench\u0002marking process are produced and communicated to the benchmarking part\u0002ners, and the improvement of the different tools is performed in several im\u0002provement cycles.\nL134@P7: While the three phases mentioned before are devoted to the tool improvement,\nL135@P7: the goal of the Recalibration task is to improve the benchmarking process itself\nL136@P7: after each benchmarking iteration, using the lessons learnt while performing the\nL137@P7: benchmarking.\nL138@P7: We are, in this report, mainly concerned with the design of the evaluation, i.e.,\nL139@P7: the Plan and Experiment phases described above. Precising how to report and com\u0002municate on the results is considered in §4; while planning the corrective methods,\nL140@P7-8: 4evaluation alignment\nL141@P8: plan\nL142@P8: experiment\nL143@P8: recalibration improve\nL144@P8: ❄\nL145@P8: ❅\nL146@P8: ❅\nL147@P8: ❅\nL148@P8: ❅❅❘\nL149@P8: ✁\nL150@P8: ✁\nL151@P8: ✁\nL152@P8: ✁\nL153@P8: ✁\nL154@P8: ✁\nL155@P8: ✁\nL156@P8: ✁\nL157@P8: ✁\nL158@P8: ✁☛✁\nL159@P8: \nL160@P8: \nL161@P8: \nL162@P8: ✠\nL163@P8: ❅\nL164@P8: ❅\nL165@P8: ❅\nL166@P8: ❅❅❘\nL167@P8: ✲ ✛\nL168@P8: Figure 1.1: The evaluation process paralleled with the development process.\nL169@P8: improving the actual systems and monitoring the results is a matter concerning al\u0002gorithms developers and is not covered in this report. However, since, we already\nL170@P8: run two evaluation events in 2004, we have implemented these two steps already\nL171@P8: and this report can be seen as the end of the recalibration phase.\nL172@P8: The remainder of this report consists in proposing mainly the main outline for\nL173@P8: the Plan phase.\nL174@P8: 1.3 Examples of evaluations\nL175@P8: In order to illustration what can be done as evaluation, we briefly present a model\nL176@P8: evaluation initiative, TREC, and the two event that we organised in 2004.\nL177@P8: 1.3.1 TREC\nL178@P8: TREC1is the “Text REtrieval Conference” organised by the NIST in the USA. It\nL179@P8: has been run yearly since 1992. It is a very good model for evaluation in a focussed\nL180@P8: research field, especially because it has been very successful.\nL181@P8: TREC goals are:\nL182@P8: – increase research in information retrieval based on large-scale collection;\nL183@P8: 1\nL184@P8: http://trec.nist.gov\nL185@P8-9: 5– provide a forum for stakeholders;\nL186@P9: – facilitate technology transfer;\nL187@P9: – improve evaluation methodology;\nL188@P9: – create a series of test collections on various aspects of IR.\nL189@P9: It is now organised in several tracks (corresponding to one kind of evaluation)\nL190@P9: which is organized over several years (5 is now the standard) for being able to\nL191@P9: compare the results. Tracks organized so far have covered:\nL192@P9: – static text retrieval;\nL193@P9: – interactive retrieval;\nL194@P9: – information retrieval in a narrow domain using ad hoc resources (genomics);\nL195@P9: – media (other than text) retrieval;\nL196@P9: – answer finding.\nL197@P9: Each track typically has between 8 and 20 participants. While each track is pre\u0002cisely defined, TREC has now a track record on investigating the evaluation of\nL198@P9: many different features of the retrieval task.\nL199@P9: 1.3.2 I3CON and EON\nL200@P9: We have organised two events in 2004 which are the premises of a larger evaluation\nL201@P9: event:\nL202@P9: – The Information Interpretation and Integration Conference (I3CON), held at\nL203@P9: the NIST Performance Metrics for Intelligent Systems (PerMIS) Workshop,\nL204@P9: is an ontology alignment demonstration competition on the model of the\nL205@P9: NIST Text Retrieval Conference. This contest has focused “real-life” test\nL206@P9: cases and comparison of algorithm global performance.\nL207@P9: – The Ontology Alignment Contest at the 3rd Evaluation of Ontology-based\nL208@P9: Tools (EON) Workshop, held at the International Semantic Web Conference\nL209@P9: (ISWC), targeted the characterisation of alignment methods with regard to\nL210@P9: particular ontology features. This contest defined a proper set of benchmark\nL211@P9: tests for assessing feature-related behavior.\nL212@P9: These two events are described more thoroughly in [Sure et al., 2004] and [Euzenat\nL213@P9: et al., 2004b].\nL214@P9: 1.4 Types of evaluations\nL215@P9: There can be several classifications of benchmarks depending on the criteria used.\nL216@P9: We can divide benchmarking with regard to what they are supposed to evaluate:\nL217@P9-10: 6competence benchmarks allows to characterise the level of competence and per\u0002formance of a particular system with regard to a set of well defined tasks.\nL218@P10: Usually, tasks are designed to isolate particular characteristics. This kind of\nL219@P10: benchmarking is relevant to kernel benchmark or unit tests;\nL220@P10: comparison benchmark allows to compare the performance of various systems\nL221@P10: on a clearly defined task or application.\nL222@P10: The goal of these two kinds of benchmarks are different: competence bench\u0002marks aim at helping system designers to evaluate their systems and to localise\nL223@P10: them which regard with a common stable framework. It is helpful for improving\nL224@P10: individual systems. The comparison benchmarks enables to compare systems with\nL225@P10: regard to each others on a general purpose tasks. Its goal is mainly to help im\u0002proving the field as a whole rather than individual systems. These two kinds of\nL226@P10: benchmarks are futher considered below.\nL227@P10: In [Castro et al., 2004], the following classification, due to [Stefani et al.,\nL228@P10: 2003], describes the four following types of benchmarks that can be used in the\nL229@P10: evaluation of software systems:\nL230@P10: Application benchmarks These benchmarks use real applications and workload\nL231@P10: conditions.\nL232@P10: Synthetic benchmarks These benchmarks emulate the functionalities of signifi\u0002cant applications, while cutting out additional or less important features.\nL233@P10: Kernel benchmarks These benchmarks use simple functions designed to repre\u0002sent key portions of real applications.\nL234@P10: Technology-specific benchmarks These benchmarks are designed to point out\nL235@P10: the main differences of devices belonging to the same technological fam\u0002ily.\nL236@P10: The I3CON experiment choose the first approach and ended with the second, while\nL237@P10: the EON initiative has used the fourth option.\nL238@P10: This classification is concerned by the way to design benchmarks while the\nL239@P10: competence /performance classification is based on what is evaluated by the bench\u0002marks. These two are not totally independent as the phrasing suggests it. Since we\nL240@P10: are first interested by the “what to evaluate” rather than the “how”, we will focus\nL241@P10: on competence/performance.\nL242@P10: 1.4.1 Competence benchmark\nL243@P10: Competence benchmarks aim at characterising the kind of task each method is\nL244@P10: good at or which kind of input it can handle well. There are many different areas\nL245@P10-11: 7in which methods can be evaluated. One of them is the kind of features they use\nL246@P11: for finding matching entities (this complements the taxonomy provided in [Rahm\nL247@P11: and Bernstein, 2001]):\nL248@P11: terminological (T) comparing the labels of the entities trying to find those which\nL249@P11: have similar names;\nL250@P11: internal structure comparison (I) comparing the internal structure of entities (e.g.,\nL251@P11: the value range or cardinality of their attributes);\nL252@P11: external structure comparison (S) comparing the relations of the entities with\nL253@P11: other entities;\nL254@P11: extensional comparison (E) comparing the known extension of entities, i.e. the\nL255@P11: set of other entities that are attached to them (in general instances of classes);\nL256@P11: semantic comparison (M) comparing the interpretations (or more exactly the mod\u0002els satisfying the entities).\nL257@P11: A set of reference benchmarks, targetting one type of feature at a time can be\nL258@P11: defined. These benchmarks would caracterize the competence of the method for\nL259@P11: one of these particular features of the languages.\nL260@P11: 1.4.2 Performance benchmarks: competition\nL261@P11: Performance benchmarks are aimed at evaluating the overall behaviour of align\u0002ment methods in versatile real-life examples. It can be organised as a yearly or\nL262@P11: bi-annual challenge (à la TREC) for comparing the best compound methods. Such\nL263@P11: benchmarks should yield as a result the distance between provided output and ex\u0002pected result as well as traditional measures of the amount of resource consumed\nL264@P11: (time, memory, user input, etc.).\nL265@P11: 1.5 Conclusion\nL266@P11: The main goal of the Ontology Alignment Evaluation Initiative is the improvement\nL267@P11: of ontology alignment techniques. For that purpose we will define the kind of tests\nL268@P11: to be processed and measures for assessing the results. This will be done for two\nL269@P11: kinds of tests: competence and performance benchmarks.\nL270@P11: Next chapter evaluates what is the variability in the alignment task, and, con\u0002sequently, what are the parameters that must be controlled in its evaluation. Chap\u0002ter 3 considers the potential evaluation metrics that can be used in order to assess\nL271@P11: the performance of the evaluated algorithms. Chapter 4 provides the definition of\nL272@P11: a possible evaluation process, including the identification of actors.\nL273@P11-12: 8The conclusion of each chapter recalls the current options retained by the On\u0002tology Alignment Evaluation Initiative.\nL274@P12: This document is largely based on Knowledge web2 deliverable 2.2.2 [Euzenat\nL275@P12: et al., 2004b]. However, it will evolve independently in function of the Ontology\nL276@P12: Alignment Evaluation Initiative.\nL277@P12: 2\nL278@P12: http://knowledgeweb.semanticweb.org\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn24view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn23view1\",\"id\":58}); Total lines: 1\n"
